package cosmonic:agent-spawn@0.1.0;

/// The caller may load a tool into itself while it runs. Not published; for
/// discussion.
///
/// Spawning is declarative: a child's tools are fixed when it starts. This
/// interface is the separate, explicit power to change a running agent's own
/// tool set. A world that does not import it cannot load anything, and a
/// spawned child never receives it in this version.
///
/// A loaded tool is a component with its own grants, started by the host.
/// The host offers its functions through the caller's own
/// `cosmonic:agent/tools` import and dispatches calls to it, so the caller
/// carries no router; that import must be served by the host for loading to
/// work. Admission follows `spawn`: the tool's reach must fit inside the
/// caller's ceiling, and anything beyond it goes to a grantor and returns
/// `needs-approval` with a digest over the caller's identity and the
/// `tool-request`. Policy may ask for approval even within the ceiling,
/// since new code is new risk even when its reach is not.
@since(version = 0.1.0)
interface tool-loading {
    use types.{tool-request, admission-error};

    /// A tool loaded into the caller. Dropping it unloads the tool: the host
    /// stops offering its functions and stops its component. A call in
    /// progress is cancelled cooperatively; its effects are not rolled back.
    /// The host also unloads every tool when the caller's store ends.
    resource loaded-tool {
        /// The names under which the caller's `tools` import offers this
        /// tool's functions. Chosen by the host, which keeps them unique.
        names: async func() -> list<string>;
    }

    /// Check the request against the ceiling and policy, start the tool, and
    /// offer it through the caller's `tools` import. Resolves once the tool
    /// is offered.
    load: async func(request: tool-request) -> result<loaded-tool, admission-error>;
}
